# Budget Allocation: The Depressed Classes
Tamil title: நிதி ஒதுக்கீடு - தாழ்த்தப்பட்டோர்

Speech No. 29, 23 March 1959

**Thiru C. N. Annadurai:** Mr. Speaker, if there is any department in our State that can be welcomed without party distinction, we recognise that it is this department engaged in advancing the people described here as the depressed classes. I believe the country will certainly welcome it. If our side has moved a cut motion on the funds allocated to this department, there is no need to explain that it is merely a formal motion, intended only to insist that more and still more must be allocated, and for no other purpose.

When we look openly at the progress these people have made under today’s Congress Government, at how far behind they formerly stood and how far they have advanced since then, I consider their progress to have been slow. The Government’s own report notes that the schemes and work in this department up to 1924 existed only in name. *[Several lines at the end of the first scanned page are damaged.]*

The report says that, even though greater financial resources are available today and some plans have been undertaken to improve within ten years the condition of these people, the work and schemes up to 1947 had existed only in name. Such a statement merely enables others, when examining what is now being done, to point out its deficiencies. If matters continue in this way and somebody issues a report in 1975 or 1977, the ruling party of that time may have to say that everything done up to 1959 existed only in name.

Therefore, when issuing reports on this subject, the ruling party should sincerely recognise that the problem is a national one and lies beyond all parties. The reports should explain that fact instead of merely proclaiming the glory of one party. I respectfully ask the Government to proceed in that manner.

The position in which one party alone could claim Mahatma Gandhi is now changing. Many people holding views opposed to Gandhi’s principles have today joined the Congress organisation, and Congress is in a position where it welcomes such people with both hands. There is no need to explain the disputes among parties in this situation. We must understand the divisions that exist in the world. Although there have been many disputes between Black and White Americans, they have regarded the advancement of the American nation as a national problem beyond a single party. In the same way, whenever the problem of our country’s depressed classes arises, it must be treated as lying beyond parties. Whether Congress, Socialist or Kazhagam, no one differs on this.

It is genuinely welcome that two crores more have been allocated today than in 1937. At the same time, if we examine what percentage of the taxes collected in 1937 was spent on this purpose, then compare how much tax is collected now and what percentage has been allocated to this department, it may be difficult to show that even one per cent is being given to it. There is no need to pursue that calculation here. I sincerely accept that the ruling party is concerned about the advancement of these people.

At the same time, it is not proper to ask, “What more would you have done if you were in power?” If the opposition once governed, the present ruling party then served in opposition, and afterward returned to office and asked, “What did you do while you governed?”, the question would have some relevance. For good or ill, the opposition parties today are not in a position to overthrow the Government and immediately assume office. When opposition members point out shortcomings in laws and programmes, it should be understood that they do so because they want more work to be done and those faults removed.

*[A culinary metaphor across the page boundary is partly damaged.]* Members have spoken as though the leaf has been spread and the food is well cooked. I merely say: cook the rice thoroughly, cut and prepare the leaf properly, draw up the schemes well, allocate the money they require, and implement them.

Just as ten, twenty or forty crores can be allocated to build dams for arid areas, and as many crores are allotted to other projects without constantly staring at the total, funds should be allocated here in the same spirit. We need a new plan to advance these people and create a new society. There is no need to be alarmed by how many crores it may cost.

The source appears to say that one and a half crores were allocated last year and two crores now. We cannot be satisfied that their condition will improve merely with this. Only if we allocate an amount comparable to, or greater than, the allocation for education can these people see some progress after another generation. If we continue at today’s pace, even a generation later the grandson of the Honourable Ezhumalai may begin by saying, “The Government has allocated six crores to this department; nevertheless...” Therefore, a substantial amount must be allotted.

When asked how this larger sum can be raised, I wish to offer some ideas from this side. It was said here that, to carry out schemes devised by the leaders of the Education Department for spreading compulsory education, educational development conferences were conducted with public cooperation and goods worth two crores were donated. In the same way, if the ruling party sincerely seeks the cooperation of other prosperous citizens, conducts conferences for the advancement of the Harijan people, and aims to raise two or three crores every year, it can certainly succeed.

When grants are allocated to education, ten or twelve crores are provided. Beyond that, desks, chairs, books and other materials are purchased. Teachers are sent out to raise funds to construct schools. Similarly, if the Government approaches the public with the cooperation and support of other parties to improve the settlements and advance these people, raising the required money is not impossible. No one need think this resembles asking for alms. Everyone will consider it an attempt to atone for a wrong that has been committed. I respectfully ask the Government to adopt this method.

I must say another thing with deep regret. I thought repeatedly about what facilities this Government has created for Harijan people to mingle with others. I need not speak about what occurs during elections. In ordinary life, what avenues have been arranged for them to associate and converse with people of other communities? At what moments are such opportunities available? *[Several lines are damaged.]* Only when avenues exist for all this will the feeling of caste gradually disappear. The Government refers to this in its report.

I said it last year and repeat it now: the observance remains a Collector’s durbar; it does not create a feeling in the mind. On Harijan Day, the minister invited to the event should not stay in the city but come to the settlements and stay with Harijan people. The minister should join them and converse with them. It should be observed in this way as a national day.

Merely catching draught cattle, branding them “H.W.,” deciding to whom they should be handed and then handing them over will not create social fellowship. I wish to say that the Government must devise a suitable programme through which people can actually mingle.

I have also observed that combining funds for Harijans and backward classes and then dividing scholarships between them creates resentment. There is no benefit in concealing it. Backward-class people argue that they are entitled to receive a larger share; Harijan people complain that money is allocated in their name but they do not receive enough. I witnessed this today.

Responsible members such as Swami Sahajananda said that because this department is held by one person, Harijan people do not benefit in the proper proportion. I am not now examining the merits of that claim. But it is very dangerous for such an ugly suspicion to grow. Merely preaching, “Do not entertain such thoughts,” will not prevent it.

I therefore offer the minister one small suggestion: why must these two departments be combined? If one separate department administers funds allotted to Harijan people and another administers funds for backward-class people, the dispute that “our money goes there” or “our money comes to you” may not arise. I firmly believe so. If suitable action can be taken, I ask that it be done.

In general, if we wish to improve the condition of these people, we must first examine the occupations in which most of them are engaged. Only then can we decide clearly whether their condition can be improved. More than ninety out of every hundred Harijan people are engaged in agriculture. That means they own no land and cultivate the land of others. If they receive land to cultivate and their productive resources grow so that they can reap and enjoy the harvest, others will not wholly disregard them.

Although there is no law requiring only members of that community to perform certain degrading occupations, there is a custom to that effect. By degrading occupations I mean sweeping refuse and removing human waste. We still see only one particular community performing them.

We keep them in occupations regarded as degrading. Even when they are agricultural labourers, we do not fix their wages or establish minimum wages for agricultural workers. We do not give them housing plots or the land they cultivate. Then, if one of them somehow completes the S.S.L.C., we award a scholarship. That is like failing to apply medicine to a wound on a bullock’s back while fitting an ornament to its horn.

If we wish to raise Harijan people from their present condition, the Cabinet should immediately undertake sound schemes to give land to those who have so far cultivated the land of others. Instead, this is entangled with many other schemes: should it be tied to the land ceiling, the land ceiling to cooperative farming, and cooperative farming to cooperatives? They keep knotting one to another, and delay deciding where to begin.

As many members have said, these people need housing plots and land to cultivate. If land cannot be supplied and they work for wages, their wages must be fixed. If they are engaged in occupations regarded as degrading, measures must be taken to remove the burden of degradation that society places upon them. If these matters are addressed immediately, their present condition will change and the country will realise how much they form its backbone.

I will proudly add one thing. Wherever they have been given an opportunity, their talent, ability and intelligence have been displayed to such a degree that members of other communities have said, “They are in no way behind us.” Despite being subjected to every sort of trial and ordeal by fire, we were deeply saddened today when we heard the manner in which Congress members spoke while our members addressed the House.

If the minister thinks I will use this to mock him, I say that instead of mockery, we should use this day to cleanse the impurities in one another’s hearts. I therefore ask that this be regarded as a national problem.

The minister said that a hostel for Harijan students would be opened in Kanchipuram and that it would bear the name “Kamakshi.” Perhaps he thought I would say I liked the hostel but disliked “Kamakshi.” Whatever its name, everyone should welcome a hostel. I tell the minister that there is already a place in Kanchipuram serving tasty food called “Kamakshi Hotel.” I therefore have no objection to the name.

I warmly welcome the proposed hostel. I thank the Honourable Kakkan that, after a long time, his benevolent glance has at least travelled a little toward Kanchipuram. With that, I conclude my speech.

---NOTES---
The historical source alternates among “தாழ்த்தப்பட்டோர்,” “பழங்குடி மக்கள்,” “அரிஜன/ஹரிஜன மக்கள்” and “பிற்படுத்தப்பட்ட வகுப்பார்.” These period terms are retained or translated contextually; they should not be read as interchangeable modern administrative categories. Several lines on Images 1, 3, 4, 5 and 6 are OCR-damaged, and only the recoverable argument is translated. The figure printed as “112 கோடி” is contextually likely “1½ கோடி,” but the source is preserved and the translation explicitly marks the reading as apparent rather than certain. “H.W.” on branded cattle is unexplained in the available text and remains unreconstructed.
